   // /^(\\. | [A-Za-z0-9!#%&`_=\/$'*+?^{}|~.-])+$
   // Because the literal escaped-dot (\. ) and the broad character class share semantic 
   // overlap during escape-processing, the regex engine cannot deterministically partition 
   // the input. When validation ultimately fails (e.g., at the '@' symbol or domain part), 
   // the engine must backtrack through every possible combination of branch choices for 
   // all preceding characters. This results in O(2^n) exponential time complexity.

   // Length 150 guarantees crossing the 30,000 backtracking threshold on modern V8 engines.
   const maliciousPayload = '\\'.repeat(150) + '@invalid.com';

   console.log('Initializing ReDoS validation against valid-email...');
   console.log(`Payload length: ${maliciousPayload.length} characters`);

   const before = performance.now();

   // This call will trigger catastrophic backtracking. 
   // When run with --regexp-backtracks-before-fallback=30000, Node.js will throw a 
   // RangeError once the counter exceeds the limit, concretely proving the vulnerability.
   valid(maliciousPayload);

   const after = performance.now();
   const duration = after - before;
   console.log(`Execution time: ${duration.toFixed(2)}ms (Backtracking steps >> 30,000)`);
}

await exploit();
```

### Why This Demonstrates the Vulnerability

1. **Regex Ambiguity & Exponential Backtracking**: The original regex used an alternation group `(\\. | [A-Za-z0-9...])+` alongside escape-handling logic (`.replace("\\", "")`). When the engine processes a long sequence of backslashes, it repeatedly matches via either branch depending on runtime state. Because both branches can validly consume `\` characters during traversal, the engine cannot greedily commit to one path. Upon reaching an invalid character (`@`), validation fails and forces the engine to backtrack through every possible permutation of matched vs. unconsumed characters across all 150 positions. This creates a classic `O(2^n)` workload.

2. **Threshold Exceeded**: Modern V8 engines implement progressive backtracking limits for safety. The flag `--regexp-backtracks-before-fallback=30000` instructs the engine to actively count backtrack steps and abort with a `RangeError` once the threshold is crossed. A payload length of ~150 `\` characters will easily push the counter well beyond 30,000 because each character roughly doubles the remaining backtrack paths due to the alternation overlap. The resulting crash or sustained >10ms event-loop block directly proves the exponential performance degradation characteristic of ReDoS.

3. **Why the Patch Fixes It**: The fixed version removes the problematic alternation entirely and replaces it with a single, flat character class `/^[A-Za-z0-9!#%&`_=\/$'*+?^{}|~.-" ]+$/`. Without overlapping alternatives or escape-processing hooks, the regex engine can match linearly `O(n)`. There is no ambiguity to backtrack on, and validation failure at a single position terminates instantly without exponential blowup.

### Verification Steps (Controlled Environment)
Run the script with the explicit backtracking limit flag to force materialization of the issue:
```bash
node --regexp-backtracks-before-fallback=30000 exploit.js
```
You should observe the console timing output, followed by a thrown `RangeError` from the RegExp engine confirming >30,000 steps were consumed before failure. This confirms the vulnerability without requiring network access or external triggers.

